# Copy to .env and fill in. .env is git-ignored and must never be committed.
# Used from later milestones (M1 planner, M5 Slack). Not required for M0.
# LLM_PROVIDER selects which client the factory builds: "anthropic" or "openai".
# Only the API key for the CHOSEN provider is required.
LLM_PROVIDER=openai
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
OPENAI_API_KEY=
SLACK_WEBHOOK_URL=

# --- Voice input. Optional: leave blank and the bar never opens the microphone. ---
# Local transcription via whisper.cpp — nothing is sent to a cloud STT service.
#   1. Binary: download a prebuilt Windows release from
#      https://github.com/ggml-org/whisper.cpp/releases and point at whisper-cli.exe
#   2. Model:  download a ggml model, e.g. ggml-base.en.bin, from
#      https://huggingface.co/ggerganov/whisper.cpp/tree/main
# Use full absolute paths.
WHISPER_EXE_PATH=
WHISPER_MODEL_PATH=
# Language passed to whisper's -l flag. "auto" lets whisper detect it.
WHISPER_LANGUAGE=en
# Optional: pin the one hotkey. Left blank, the app claims the first FREE combo from
# Ctrl+Shift+Space → Ctrl+Alt+Space → Ctrl+Alt+M → Alt+Shift+Space and logs which it got.
# (Ctrl+Alt+Space is taken by the Microsoft IME on some Windows installs.)
HOTKEY=

# --- System-wide dictation (M12/M12.1). Requires WHISPER_EXE_PATH/WHISPER_MODEL_PATH above —
# voice off means dictation is off too, since there is no separate transcription path. Hold
# nothing: tap the hotkey to start listening, press ENTER to stop and type the transcript
# into whatever window currently has focus, in ANY app. Never goes through the planner. ---
# Optional: pin the dictation hotkey. Left blank, the app claims the first FREE combo from
# Ctrl+M → Ctrl+Shift+Alt+D → Ctrl+Alt+D → Ctrl+Alt+J and logs which it got. Deliberately NOT
# Ctrl+Alt+V (Excel's Paste Special) or Alt+Shift+D (Word's insert-date field) — both are
# real, common conflicts in exactly the apps dictation is most likely used in.
DICTATE_HOTKEY=

# --- Gmail reply tools (M10). Optional: leave blank and they are never offered at all. ---
# DOM-based control of Gmail in Chrome, over the Chrome DevTools Protocol — no screenshots,
# no coordinate clicking. Chrome must be started with remote debugging AND a dedicated
# profile directory: since Chrome 136 the debugging port is refused on the default profile.
#
#   chrome.exe --remote-debugging-port=9222 --user-data-dir="%LOCALAPPDATA%\voice-agent-chrome"
#
# Sign in to Gmail once in that window (a test account first — sendReply really does send).
CHROME_DEBUG_URL=http://127.0.0.1:9222

# --- Google Calendar (M13). Optional: leave blank and the three calendar tools are never
# offered at all. Unlike Gmail/Notion this is a real API, not a browser — a calendar event is
# structured data, so there is no live draft box a DOM would buy anything for. ---
#
# ONE-TIME SETUP
#   1. https://console.cloud.google.com → new project → APIs & Services
#   2. Enable the "Google Calendar API".
#   3. OAuth consent screen: External. Add yourself as a test user.
#   4. Credentials → Create credentials → OAuth client ID → "Desktop app".
#      Paste the client ID and secret below.
#   5. Run:  npm run calendar:connect
#      It asks for ONE scope: calendar.events — enough to read, create and move events, and
#      nothing more. (That is also why the app reads the calendar's timezone out of the events
#      listing rather than from /calendars/primary, which this scope does not cover.)
#      It opens your normal browser, and prints a refresh token to paste in below.
#      Then RESTART the app — the token is read once, at startup.
#
# TWO THINGS THAT LOOK LIKE BUGS AND ARE NOT:
#
#   * "Google hasn't verified this app" at consent time. Calendar is a "sensitive" scope, and
#     clearing that warning needs full Google verification — domain ownership, a published
#     privacy policy, a review — which is not worth pursuing for a personal project. Click
#     "Advanced", then "Go to <your app name>". Expected, every time you reconnect.
#
#   * Access silently dying after 7 days. A Cloud project left in "Testing" publishing status
#     expires refresh tokens after a week. Set the project to "In production" on the OAuth
#     consent screen — an unverified app can still be used by its own owner, and the tokens
#     stop expiring. The app will say "expired" and tell you to reconnect if this bites you.
#
# GOOGLE_REFRESH_TOKEN is a SECRET — it is the entire connection to your calendar. Never
# committed, never logged, never included in an error message.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
GOOGLE_REFRESH_TOKEN=

# --- Voice OUTPUT (M14, optional) ------------------------------------------------------
#
# Unset means the app simply does not speak — exactly the way unset whisper paths mean it
# does not listen. Nothing else changes: the `elaborate` tool is not even offered to the
# model, because with no synthesizer nothing ever fills the store it reads from.
#
# Piper is local: no API key, no account, nothing leaves the machine. Two ways to get it,
# and the app does not care which — it spawns whatever binary this path points at:
#
#   pip install piper-tts                 -> <venv>\Scripts\piper.exe   (maintained, v1.7.x)
#   rhasspy/piper v1.2.0 windows zip      -> piper\piper.exe            (archived, no Python)
#
# Then download a voice ONCE, ahead of time:
#
#   python -m piper.download_voices en_US-amy-medium
#
# Downloading it up front is deliberate, not incidental: a feature whose whole premise is
# "nothing leaves the machine" must not make a network call on its first utterance.
PIPER_EXE_PATH=
PIPER_MODEL_PATH=

# --- Pointing (M16, optional) ------------------------------------------------------------
#
# "Where's the send button?" -> the app reads the controls of the window you were looking at,
# asks a model which one you meant, and draws a marker over it. It NEVER clicks: you do.
#
# NO SCREENSHOT IS TAKEN. Through M15 this worked by photographing your screen and asking a
# vision model to find the thing in the picture; live testing found it pointing at the WRONG
# CONTROL on ordinary Windows chrome — one tab over, ~200px onto a neighbouring icon — so the
# grounding moved to Windows' own accessibility layer. That machinery is gone, not disabled,
# and VISION_ENABLED / VISION_PROVIDER / VISION_MODEL no longer do anything.
#
# WHAT ACTUALLY LEAVES YOUR MACHINE: the NAMES of the controls in ONE window, as text — "File",
# "Edit", "Advice.txt", "New" — plus what you asked for. The model's whole answer is a number
# picking one entry off that list. Every coordinate is read from Windows and never comes from a
# model, which is the entire point of the redesign.
#
# It reads the window that was in front when you pressed the hotkey, captured before the command
# bar takes focus. Not the desktop, not your other windows, and never this app's own UI. With
# this unset, no window is ever read and no reader process is started.
#
# It still has a flag of its own rather than switching on when it finds an API key: a smaller
# disclosure is still a disclosure, and "I configured an LLM" must not silently mean "I consented
# to the app reading my windows".
#
# Set to exactly 1 to turn it on.
POINTING_ENABLED=

# --- Opening apps (M18). Optional: the four built-ins work without this. ---
#
# "Open Spotify", "open notepad", "open calc", "open files" work out of the box — Spotify,
# Notepad, Calculator and File Explorer are built in. This setting adds YOUR apps to that list.
#
# The model never supplies a path or a command. It passes the name you said, and the app
# matches it against this closed list. That is why there is no fuzzy matching: a name one typo
# off is refused with the list of what CAN be opened, because launching the wrong program is
# something you find out about by watching it open.
#
# Form:  Name=command|Name2=command2       (pipe-separated, one = per entry)
#
# A command must be ONE of:
#   * a bare executable name          vlc.exe
#   * an absolute path with a drive   C:\Program Files\VideoLAN\VLC\vlc.exe
#     letter (quotes optional)        "C:\Program Files\VideoLAN\VLC\vlc.exe"
#   * a bare protocol scheme          slack:
#
# AND NO ARGUMENTS. `notepad.exe /A` is rejected, not silently truncated. Apps are launched
# with no shell, so an argument string would be handed to the program as one literal argv entry
# rather than parsed — almost never what you meant. Launching with arguments is out of scope.
#
# Also rejected: relative paths, UNC paths (\\server\share\x.exe), protocol URIs that carry a
# payload (slack://channel/general), http:/https:/file:/javascript:/data:/vbscript: (use
# openTarget for a website), and any name that collides with a built-in or with an earlier
# entry here — including via an alias, so `calc=...` is refused.
#
# A malformed entry is REPORTED at startup and never launched. Watch the console on first run:
# a line beginning "[main] I ignored the APPS_EXTRA ..." tells you exactly which entry and why.
#
# Example:
# APPS_EXTRA=VLC=C:\Program Files\VideoLAN\VLC\vlc.exe|Slack=slack:|VS Code=code.exe
APPS_EXTRA=

# --- Spotify (M18). Optional: leave blank and the two Spotify tools are never offered. ---
#
# "Play Bohemian Rhapsody on Spotify", "turn the volume up". The Web API, not the desktop app's
# own controls — so it works on whatever device Spotify is already playing on, and it needs no
# browser and no screen reading.
#
# NOTE THE SPLIT: opening the Spotify APP is `openApp` and needs none of this (see the "Opening
# apps" section above). This is only for controlling PLAYBACK. "Play X on Spotify" with Spotify
# closed is refused with "open Spotify first" rather than launching it — starting an application
# is not something the app does as a side effect of being asked to play a song.
#
# PREMIUM IS REQUIRED, TWICE OVER, AND THIS IS THE THING TO CHECK FIRST:
#   * Spotify's playback-control endpoints only work for Premium accounts.
#   * An app in "development mode" (which is what yours will be — see quotas below) additionally
#     requires THE APP OWNER'S OWN ACCOUNT to be Premium for the app to function at all.
# On a free account expect the app to say so in as many words. That is a real, named failure,
# not a generic error.
#
# ONE-TIME SETUP
#   1. https://developer.spotify.com/dashboard -> Create app
#   2. Redirect URI — paste EXACTLY this, including the port and /callback:
#
#        http://127.0.0.1:8724/callback
#
#      NOT http://localhost:8724/callback. Spotify's redirect-URI rules changed on 2025-04-09
#      (all clients migrated by November 2025): `localhost` is explicitly rejected, and a
#      loopback redirect must use the literal IP — 127.0.0.1 or [::1]. Everything else has to
#      be HTTPS. This is the single most likely thing to go wrong here, and it fails with
#      "INVALID_CLIENT: Invalid redirect URI".
#      (If port 8724 is taken, set SPOTIFY_REDIRECT_PORT below and register that port instead.)
#   3. Which API/SDKs: tick "Web API".
#   4. Copy the Client ID below. THERE IS NO CLIENT SECRET TO COPY — this uses PKCE, because a
#      desktop app cannot keep a secret, and storing something that is not actually secret is
#      worse than having nothing to store.
#   5. Settings -> User Management: your own Spotify account must be on the list.
#   6. Run:  npm run spotify:connect
#      It asks for exactly two scopes — user-read-playback-state and user-modify-playback-state
#      — and nothing else. No playlists, no library, no listening history: M18's out-of-scope
#      list is enforced at the grant, not just in the code. It opens your normal browser and
#      prints a refresh token to paste in below. Then RESTART the app — the token is read once,
#      at startup.
#
# QUOTAS, which matter more than they sound: a new app is in "development mode", capped at 5
# authenticated users who must each be added by hand in User Management. Extended quota mode has
# been restricted since 2025-05-15 to registered organisations with a launched service and 250k+
# monthly active users, so a personal project stays in development mode permanently. That is
# fine for one person's own account and is never going to be fine for anything else.
#
# SPOTIFY_REFRESH_TOKEN is a SECRET — it is the entire connection to your Spotify account. Never
# committed, never logged, never included in an error message.
SPOTIFY_CLIENT_ID=
SPOTIFY_REFRESH_TOKEN=
# Optional: only if 127.0.0.1:8724 is taken on your machine. Must match the redirect URI you
# registered in the dashboard.
SPOTIFY_REDIRECT_PORT=

# --- Linear, over MCP (M19). Optional: leave blank and the Linear tools are never offered. ---
#
# The first CONNECTOR: an app reached over the Model Context Protocol through a generic adapter,
# rather than a hand-built integration like Gmail or Calendar. See README "Setting up Linear".
#
#   1. Linear -> Settings -> Security & access -> Personal API keys -> New API key.
#      It needs read AND write: creating an issue is a write.
#   2. Paste it below.
#   3. Set "defaultTeam" in connectors.json (repo root) to the exact name of the team new issues
#      should be filed under. Which tools are exposed is decided in that file too - it holds no
#      secrets and is committed. This file holds the key and is not.
#   4. Restart the app - the key is read once, at startup.
#
# What is on the menu is decided from connectors.json and whether this variable is set. No
# network call is involved, and the connection opens only when a Linear tool is first used.
#
# LINEAR_API_KEY is a SECRET - it carries your full Linear account access. Never committed,
# never logged, never included in an error message (a rejected key is reported by the NAME of
# this variable, and nothing the server said is repeated).
LINEAR_API_KEY=
